\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The circular-Bessel basis functions used for the Hubble PSFs.}}{32}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Optiland ray trace of the Hubble model. Likelihood evaluation uses circular-Bessel coefficients at five field coordinates (see text).\relax }}{33}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Nautilus posterior corner plot for the five secondary-misalignment parameters of the Hubble model (axes labelled $dx$, $dy$, $dz$, $tx$, $ty$ correspond to $sdx$, $sdy$, $sdz$, $stx$, $sty$). Titles give the full-posterior marginal medians.\relax }}{34}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Hubble benchmark with noisy PSFs: on-axis PSF and RMS wavefront error (WFE; the on-axis OPD RMS quoted in the text) as the alignment is refined. (a) PSFs for the uncorrected system (the observed PSF, with Gaussian noise of $\sigma =0.01\%$ of the ideal peak), the Nautilus starting point (median of the final 100 posterior samples), DE solutions at generations 10, 17, 21 and 30 (A--D) and the DE solution after 2000 generations (final). (b) RMS WFE of the DE best estimate versus generation. (c) DE best estimate of each secondary-mirror parameter versus generation, with the injected truth.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Residual on-axis OPD maps of the Hubble model: the difference between the nominal design and the system with residual misalignment $\boldsymbol {\theta }_{\mathrm {true}}-\mathbf {c}$, for the uncorrected system, the Nautilus starting point and the DE solution after 2000 generations. Piston is removed and tilt is retained, as in the quoted RMS WFE. Each panel has its own symmetric color scale in waves, with zero in gray. By variance, the final residual is 57\% defocus and 39\% tilt.\relax }}{36}{figure.caption.20}%
